## [ISSUE] Missing `key` on `GrafanaRuleListItem` inside `rules.map`

**Severity:** MEDIUM — causes a React key warning, and the incrementally loaded filter results are reconciled by index, so component state such as an open menu, silence drawer or delete modal can move to the wrong rule.

### The Problem

```tsx
case 'grafana':
  return (
    <GrafanaRuleListItem
      rule={rule}
      groupIdentifier={groupIdentifier}
      namespaceName={ruleWithOrigin.namespaceName}
      showLocation={true}
    />
  );
```

The previous `<GrafanaRuleLoader key={key} ... />` had a key. The `datasource` and `default` branches still pass `key={key}`.

### Theory / Proof

React needs a stable `key` on each element returned from `Array.prototype.map`. Without one it falls back to the array index and logs "Each child in a list should have a unique key prop" (https://react.dev/learn/rendering-lists#keeping-list-items-in-order-with-key). `FilterView` appends results as the generator yields them and supports "load more". Each `RuleActionsButtons` holds local state (`showSilenceDrawer`, `redirectToClone`, the delete modal), and index-based reconciliation can attach that state to a different rule.

### Recommendation

```tsx
<GrafanaRuleListItem
  key={key}
  rule={rule}
  groupIdentifier={groupIdentifier}
  namespaceName={ruleWithOrigin.namespaceName}
  showLocation={true}
/>
```
